INT. COMMUNITY BOXING GYM, BASEMENT - NIGHT

Cinderblock walls. A single ring with sagging ropes. Hand-lettered signs: NO EGOS. NO EXCUSES. A speed bag hangs motionless. The smell of liniment and old wet leather.

COACH BERNADETTE KOWALSKI-OSEI (60s, a woman who looks like she's been punched by every decade) leans against the ring post, a towel around her neck. Inside the ring, in a cold-sweat silence, stands DESHAWN "DEE" THOMPSON-ABARA (20s, wiry, wrapped hands, eyes on the floor) with his shoulders hunched.

BERNADETTE
Fourth round. Last one.

DEE
I know.

BERNADETTE
You're ahead on cards.

DEE
I know.

BERNADETTE
You do nothing but stay ahead, you win.

DEE
I know, Coach.

BERNADETTE
Then why do you look like you're about to be sick?

He doesn't answer. He lifts his gloves; she grabs the tape from the rope and tightens one wrist, then the other; he flexes his fingers; she taps his chest twice with the flat of her hand; and he exhales, a long shaky breath that fogs the cold air.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
Look at me.

DEE
I'm looking.

BERNADETTE
You're looking at my forehead.

DEE
It's a good forehead.

BERNADETTE
Dee.

He raises his eyes. She holds them.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
When the bell rings, you don't think about the title. You don't think about your father. You think about the jab.

DEE
The jab.

BERNADETTE
Just the jab.

DEE
That's all?

BERNADETTE
Jab, step, jab. Pivot. Jab.

DEE
And if he hits me?

BERNADETTE
He'll hit you.

DEE
And?

BERNADETTE
And you'll get hit. And you'll jab.

A buzzer sounds from somewhere across the hall. Dee jerks. Bernadette doesn't blink. Her hand slides under his chin, tilts his face up, and settles him with a pat like a father's.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
Go.

He ducks through the ropes. She swings the stool into the corner, wedges the water bottle between her knees, and the bell clangs.

Dee steps into the center. Gloves up. And for the first time all night, he smiles.
